REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w08","title":"Statement of purpose, précis and lab reports","summary":"Three kinds of careful writing. A statement of purpose (SOP) tells your own story in simple words; a précis says everything a passage says in one-third of its words; a lab report records what was done, how, and what happened, in impersonal language.","n":8,"lectures":[{"code":"8.1","title":"Statement of purpose (sop)- I","video":"iKR77w_881A","seconds":2152},{"code":"8.2","title":"Statement of purpose (sop)-II | clarity, brevity & self-revision for impactful writing","video":"aUWOaB8M5sc","seconds":1596},{"code":"8.3","title":"Precis writing- I | steps, rules & techniques for clear and concise summaries","video":"k7H0g4DdLrQ","seconds":1082},{"code":"8.4","title":"Precis writing- II | techniques, rules & practice for concise and clear summaries","video":"BT8OixxCEU4","seconds":830},{"code":"8.5","title":"Writing lab report","video":"YXaf1DGYAus","seconds":1380}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"statement-of-purpose-what-it-is-for","level":2,"title":"Statement of purpose: what it is for"},{"id":"what-each-paper-tells-the-reader","level":3,"title":"What each paper tells the reader"},{"id":"a-sop-or-an-sop","level":3,"title":"“A SOP” or “an SOP”?"},{"id":"vague-claims-and-the-evidence-behind-them","level":3,"title":"Vague claims and the evidence behind them"},{"id":"statement-of-purpose-stories-not-labels","level":2,"title":"Statement of purpose: stories, not labels"},{"id":"a-list-of-achievements-weak","level":3,"title":"A list of achievements (weak)"},{"id":"a-picture-without-a-label-strong","level":3,"title":"A picture without a label (strong)"},{"id":"more-pairs-from-the-lecture","level":3,"title":"More pairs from the lecture"},{"id":"from-below-average-to-goldman-sachs-strong","level":3,"title":"From below average to Goldman Sachs (strong)"},{"id":"what-you-learnt-outside-the-classroom","level":3,"title":"What you learnt outside the classroom"},{"id":"statement-of-purpose-simple-words-fewer-words","level":2,"title":"Statement of purpose: simple words, fewer words"},{"id":"write-for-every-reader","level":3,"title":"Write for every reader"},{"id":"great-writers-use-simple-words","level":3,"title":"Great writers use simple words"},{"id":"what-simple-language-is","level":3,"title":"What simple language is"},{"id":"uncommon-words-and-familiar-words","level":3,"title":"Uncommon words and familiar words"},{"id":"fewer-words-cut-prune-redraft","level":3,"title":"Fewer words: cut, prune, redraft"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-activity-what-can-go","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s activity: what can go?"},{"id":"pre-cis-what-it-is-and-the-two-rules","level":2,"title":"Précis: what it is and the two rules"},{"id":"jefferson-in-12-words-the-first-example","level":3,"title":"Jefferson in 12 words: the first example"},{"id":"a-longer-passage-153-words-into-51","level":3,"title":"A longer passage: 153 words into 51"},{"id":"a-pre-cis-is-a-paragraph","level":3,"title":"A précis is a paragraph"},{"id":"pre-cis-the-techniques","level":2,"title":"Précis: the techniques"},{"id":"step-1-read-and-list-the-points","level":3,"title":"Step 1: read and list the points"},{"id":"step-2-seven-techniques","level":3,"title":"Step 2: seven techniques"},{"id":"redundant-words-and-one-word-for-many","level":3,"title":"Redundant words and one word for many"},{"id":"the-drafts-49-words-into-17","level":3,"title":"The drafts: 49 words into 17"},{"id":"long-lists-of-illustrations","level":3,"title":"Long lists of illustrations"},{"id":"writing-a-lab-report","level":2,"title":"Writing a lab report"},{"id":"manual-before-report-after","level":3,"title":"Manual before, report after"},{"id":"what-a-report-answers","level":3,"title":"What a report answers"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-report-making-tea","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s report: making tea"},{"id":"the-language-of-a-report","level":3,"title":"The language of a report"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What an SOP is for</strong> (8.1): mark sheets tell what exams you passed; testimonials tell how others see you. A <strong>statement of purpose</strong> is your chance to tell the story behind your successes and failures, which only you know. Good grades get you shortlisted; among the A graders, a good SOP decides.</li>\n<li><strong>How to write an SOP</strong> (8.1): tell stories; describe specific events, incidents and targets; avoid jargon, cliché, vagueness and ambiguity; use simple words and simple sentences; be brief and error-free. Show, do not label: the strongest SOP in the lecture never says “poor”, yet the picture comes out. Tell what you learnt from family, sports, friends and teachers, with honesty and humility.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple and short</strong> (8.2): an SOP is a story, not a research paper, and its readers may come from other fields. A <strong>simple sentence</strong> has one verb; a <strong>simple word</strong> has one or two syllables and a well-known meaning. Cut redundant words (<em>a valuable asset</em>, <em>complete dedication</em>) and write two or three drafts to fit the word limit.</li>\n<li><strong>What a précis is</strong> (8.3): a <strong>précis</strong> says all that the passage says, in <strong>one-third of its words</strong> (99 words → at most 33). First read carefully and list the points; then rewrite them as one connected paragraph, draft after draft (34 words → 19 → 17 → 14 → 12).</li>\n<li><strong>Précis techniques</strong> (8.4): remove redundant words; use one word for many (<em>in our time</em> → <em>presently</em>); rewrite sentences; use indirect speech in the third person (<em>we</em> → <em>they</em>); drop long lists (<em>Chicago, Los Angeles, New York</em> → <em>here or there</em>); join sentences with a few discourse markers; write numbers above 9 as numerals (<em>36</em> is one word).</li>\n<li><strong>Lab reports</strong> (8.5): a <strong>manual</strong> comes before the work (what to do and how); a <strong>report</strong> comes after it (what was done, why, how, time and cost, results, previous work, future work). A report is concrete and specific; it describes rather than discusses; and it is impersonal: simple sentences in the <strong>passive voice</strong>, usually in the past tense, so that anyone can repeat the work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"statement-of-purpose-what-it-is-for\">Statement of purpose: what it is for</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When you apply to a university, a company or a research project, you send mark sheets, grade cards, degree certificates and <strong>testimonials</strong> (letters from teachers, employers or project leaders about you). So why do they also ask for a <strong>statement of purpose</strong>? Because all those papers show what others see. Only you can tell <strong>why</strong> things went the way they did: why you did well in one subject and badly in another, what you enjoy, how you learnt. The SOP “is intended to tell the reader who you think you are”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Statement of purpose (SOP)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>statement of purpose</strong> is a short piece of writing sent with an application. It “tells the reader who you are” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=39s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:39</a>, and it shows why you deserve the position “through points illustrated with stories, anecdotes, accounts of events” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>. An <strong>anecdote</strong> is a short true story about something that happened.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s first slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=39s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:39</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>Writing an SOP is an important skill.</li>\n<li>SOP tells the reader who you are.</li>\n<li>Good universities, companies, etc ask for an SOP. It helps them get a better picture of the candidate.</li>\n<li>Many people do not write a good SOP.</li>\n<li>They pack their SOP with vague statements meaning nothing in particular.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"what-each-paper-tells-the-reader\">What each paper tells the reader</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>What it tells the reader</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mark sheets, grade cards, degree certificates</td>\n<td>what exams you passed and how well you did</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests such as GRE, TOEFL, IELTS</td>\n<td>something about your academic ability, but not “the whole picture”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testimonials</td>\n<td>how others (employers, teachers, project leaders) see you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SOP</strong></td>\n<td>the story behind your successes and failures, which only you can tell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=744s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:24</a> ends: “Many things in life result in one way when they were actually intended to become another. But who can tell that story to others. Others only see what they have.” The lecturer’s examples: you may have done badly in a subject because it was beyond you, because the teacher was not helpful, or because something urgent happened the night before the exam. Others can only guess (<em>bad student</em>, <em>careless</em>, <em>not interested</em>); you know the reason. The SOP is your chance to tell it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why toppers need an SOP (the lecture’s numbers at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Take one university with 19 faculties. Each faculty has about 4 batches, and every batch has a topper.</li>\n<li>Toppers in one university: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">19</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">76</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>India has about 2,000 universities (the lecturer’s figure). Universities in Europe, North America and Southeast Asia take students from all over the world, and “it is only toppers that apply to them”.</li>\n<li>So the reader must choose “from hundreds of toppers”. Your grade card says you are a topper; so do all the others.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> grades alone cannot separate the candidates. The SOP is where you show why <strong>you</strong> deserve the position best. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">An SOP does not replace good grades</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“I am not saying that a good SOP can be a substitute for a good grade card ... that is the basic fundamental requirement. Your application would not be shortlisted unless you are an A grade. So be an A grade, but among the A graders, your chance lies in a good SOP.” In over 40 years of teaching, the lecturer has seen students with a good SOP, but without the very highest grades, get into good universities and companies. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-sop-or-an-sop\">“A SOP” or “an SOP”?</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an before an abbreviation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Choose <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> by the first <strong>sound</strong>, not the first letter. In <em>SOP</em> we say the letter names, and the letter S is read “ess”, which begins with a vowel sound: so <strong>an SOP</strong>. If SOP were read as one word, like <em>sop</em> or <em>soap</em>, it would begin with a consonant sound, and we would say <em>a SOP</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two more from the lecture’s own slides: <strong>an NGO</strong> (N is read “en”, a vowel sound) and <strong>a PhD</strong> (P is read “pee”, a consonant sound).</p>\n<h3 id=\"vague-claims-and-the-evidence-behind-them\">Vague claims and the evidence behind them</h3>\n<p>Many writers fill their SOP with “cliché and jargon and very general kinds of things”, such as <em>I am a hard-working person, I have a passion for hard work</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four faults to avoid</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cliché</strong>: a phrase used so often that it no longer says anything (<em>I have a passion for hard work</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Jargon</strong>: the special words of one field; people from other fields may not understand them, or may read them in another way.</li>\n<li><strong>Vagueness</strong>: words that could mean almost any amount (<em>hard-working</em>, <em>challenging assignments</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Ambiguity</strong>: words that can be read in two or more ways (<em>English postgraduate</em>, below).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Hard work” is <strong>subjective</strong> (it depends on the person): for some people, three hours at a table is hard work; for others, thirteen hours is not enough. Such words “mean nothing, unless you support these statements with at least anecdotal evidence if not statistical”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vague claim</th>\n<th>With evidence (the lecture’s versions)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I love hard work.</em></td>\n<td>“If I am awake for 18 hours, I like to work for 14.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I have always been curious.</em></td>\n<td>“Even if I was an average student, I had a curiosity to learn and while I was still in the primary school, I went and asked my teacher why the rainbow would not appear every day.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s rules, on the slide “How do we write a good SOP” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=672s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:12</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It should tell stories.</li>\n<li>It should describe specific events, incidents and targets.</li>\n<li>Avoid jargon, cliché, vagueness, ambiguity, etc.</li>\n<li>It should be written in simple words and simple sentences.</li>\n<li>It should be brief, should be error-free: no errors of grammar, punctuation, vocabulary or spelling.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A spoken SOP</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture plays two minutes of Barack Obama’s keynote address at the Democratic National Convention in Boston (from CNN). The lecturer’s point: few people knew Obama then, yet within months he won enough support to defeat a far better-known candidate. “A good SOP does that, whether verbal or written.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=771s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:51</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"statement-of-purpose-stories-not-labels\">Statement of purpose: stories, not labels</h2>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=858s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:18</a>: “Story is an important part of the SOP. But lots of people say nothing other than general statements applicable to everyone.” The lecture then compares weak and strong SOPs. The strong ones come from successful applications.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-list-of-achievements-weak\">A list of achievements (weak)</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I love to work with people and contribute to social development, especially in the areas of climate change, environmental policy and sustainability. My involvement in the process of the United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development and study of multilateral environmental agreements has equipped me with the necessary knowledge and skills. Masters research at the University of Illinois and the experience at The Energy and Resources Institute (TERI) have helped me grow into an independent researcher and team player. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=927s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:27</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>“Wonderful words, big words. But what picture do we get?” She loves to work with people and worked on a UN project. The reader wants to know how she worked with people of other cultures, languages, accents, habits and opinions, and made a team that finished the job. “That story is missing.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></li>\n<li>“It goes from point to point, it goes from peak to peak, but ... we do not get any picture of the valley which lies in between.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></li>\n<li>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1083s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:03</a>: “This information is already there on this candidate’s CV and in the testimonials that the writer’s teachers and project leaders have already given her. She could have illustrated the point and she could have told the reader about some people she worked with but without that it is a weak SOP.” (A <strong>CV</strong>, curriculum vitae, is a list of your education, work and achievements.) Saying again what the CV says is “a chance wasted”.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"a-picture-without-a-label-strong\">A picture without a label (strong)</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I come from a lower-middle class Indian family. My father is a travelling salesman, selling office stationery, and my mother supervises the work of public toilet cleaners on behalf of an NGO in Chennai. An uncle secured me a seat at the Kendriya Vidyalaya, Indian Institute of Technology Campus, Chennai, an inexpensive middle class school. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1152s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:12</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1251s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:51</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>The reader gets a picture here that the writer comes from humble background.</li>\n<li>Writer doesn’t have to use that word, I grew up in a ‘poor’ family but the picture comes out.</li>\n<li>This was part of a successful application. Though not a topper, the writer made it to a top university.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecturer adds: the facts also show <strong>how</strong> she got there (ambitious parents, a helpful uncle, a school willing to take a child from outside the campus); she remembers her background with gratitude and is determined to do well; and the university she joined has a world ranking under 20. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Show, do not label</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Do not write the label (<em>poor</em>, <em>hard-working</em>, <em>curious</em>). Write the facts and the story, and the reader will put the label on you.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"more-pairs-from-the-lecture\">More pairs from the lecture</h3>\n<p><strong>No illustration</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1296s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:36</a>: “As a child I was fond of Lego technique. I like the idea of making something with my hands.” The slide: “There is no illustration. Nothing to say why these writers say what they say.” What did the child make? The SOP does not say.</p>\n<p><strong>A candid story</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1353s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:33</a> (the slide highlights the parts in bold):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Growing up, I always loved math and sciences. I was <strong>that weird kid</strong> that liked calculus and algebra, and I thought that chemistry was fun. And because I excelled in those things, <strong>my parents always told me: “Anita, the best way to use those interests and apply them to a career, is to become an engineer.”</strong> After three years of undergraduate Computer Science Engineering, I realized that my parents were wrong in shaping my calling. <strong>Suddenly one day after seeing blue cabbage turning red in lemon sauce, I realized that it was Chemistry rather than Coding that was my calling and life-long desire.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecturer calls it “very candid”: it tells why she did not do well in engineering and why she hopes to do better in the natural sciences. (<strong>Candid</strong> means honest and open.) One specific moment, the cabbage changing colour, turns a general claim into a story.</p>\n<p><strong>General words again</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1371s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:51</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I believe in doing the job assigned to me with complete dedication, enjoy challenging assignments and looking forward to have an opportunity to prove myself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am a young and energetic English post graduate with a proven track record of academic and co-curricular achievements in some of India’s finest educational institutions. My seventeen years of teaching experience and the ability to lead and control various departments can surely add value to your esteemed organization. At present I am working at the Leo Academy and my superiors consider me as a valuable asset for the organization.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>“Where is the illustration?” The lecturer’s questions <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Complete dedication</em>: “That is why we have been hired.”</li>\n<li><em>English postgraduate</em>: English by nationality, or a postgraduate in the English language, or in English literature?</li>\n<li><em>Young and energetic</em>: are not all young people energetic?</li>\n<li><em>Proven track record</em>: proved against what?</li>\n<li><em>Seventeen years of teaching</em>: “how was one year different from another?” Did you try a new method or material, even one that did not work? “All our experiments do not always end in the results that are desired by experimenters.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A grammar slip (my note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>looking forward to have</em> should be <em>looking forward to <strong>having</strong></em>: after <em>look forward to</em>, use the <em>-ing</em> form. An SOP must be error-free (rule 5).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"from-below-average-to-goldman-sachs-strong\">From below average to Goldman Sachs (strong)</h3>\n<p>One writer’s story, told in parts <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1524s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:24</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In Std 10 I worked harder than usual and passed the public examination with credit. However, I did not realize one thing. Now I could choose the subjects I would study and I chose commerce. Then, I did not know that by choosing what I liked, I would develop an interest in my studies and thus started my journey of studying what I loved…</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slide’s verdict: “Anyone would love to take this writer.” It is a “very frank” admission, made “without boasting, without being vain”: the start of an ordinary student’s change into an extraordinary one. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was an average or even a below average student in many subjects, not getting even thirty out of a hundred in subjects like Mathematics… <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1632s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:12</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was also among the twenty five out of three hundred students that were selected by the multinational company Goldman Sachs at my college in 2008, and among the five that survived their training and the first year of work… <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1665s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:45</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How strong is “25 out of 300”? (the lecture’s numbers)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Selected: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">300</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">25</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">12</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.083</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> about <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8%</span></span></span></span>.</span> The lecturer says “top 8 per cent”.</li>\n<li>The lecturer adds that the starting salary was about 600,000 rupees a year (6 lakh), plus perks.</li>\n<li>Still there after the first year: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">300</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">60</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.017</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> under <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8056em;vertical-align:-0.0556em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2%</span></span></span></span></span> of her class (the lecturer says about 1.5 per cent).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the same writer who scored under 30 in Mathematics ended in the top 2% of her class. Her honesty about the weak start makes the success believable, and the reader asks: “how was that possible?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-you-learnt-outside-the-classroom\">What you learnt outside the classroom</h3>\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1734s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:54</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1896s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:36</a>: “We learn in many ways, from many places and many people. There are many things we learn from our families, from friends rather than only from school or college. But few of us share this story in our SOP.” And: “Anyone evaluating your application would like to know if your learning is limited to classrooms, or if you are one of those people who learn from every possible opportunity.” Given the choice, anyone would take a person who learns all through life over one who learnt only at school.</p>\n<p>The successful SOP of the salesman’s daughter draws on four sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Family</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1824s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:24</a>: “My family has taught me to respect the elders regardless of their weaknesses. It is because of my family that I am a hard working, confident individual. To give a small example about this. It has taught me the value of physical work and even sports. I am better than average shuttle badminton player.” Even the label <em>hard working, confident</em> comes with “a small example”.</li>\n<li><strong>Sports</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>: “Sports has taught me perseverance. I used to play mostly against my sister who then played better than I did. But I gradually improved and I am amused to note that on days when I was confident that I would win the match, no matter how low my score was, I managed to win it. I also represented my school at a regional event. However, on days when I thought I couldn’t make it to a ‘winning position’, I would lose.” (<strong>Perseverance</strong>: going on in spite of difficulty.)</li>\n<li><strong>Friends</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1965s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:45</a>: “I have few but very good friends, and they come from different backgrounds. A close friend is a daughter of a sanitary worker, and another comes from the family of an army officer. We often go to the beach or to play badminton or to movies, etc. They are both equally dear to me and have taught me that an individual is worthy of love and respect because of her own attributes rather than because of their parents’.” The lecturer calls the last sentence a “wonderful quotable quote”.</li>\n<li><strong>Teachers</strong> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=2022s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:42</a>: “I still miss some of my teachers from the school. For an average girl like me, it was a great experience to have been spotted by a teacher for my ability to speak and to have been made the deputy school pupil leader of the entire school having over 2000 students. Mrs... has printed on my heart that no individual is without something excellent and special, all we need to do is to recognise it.”</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Neither proud nor too humble</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many of us keep these stories to ourselves, “mostly out of ignorance, partly ... out of humility”. Others boast: “I am a class by myself; I have always stood first”. But many others have also always stood first, “and none of those toppers has the right to boast”: you are what you are because of circumstances and many factors. Write “with honesty, with frankness, with humility and in simple language”. <strong>Candour</strong> is honest, open speech; <strong>humility</strong> is not thinking yourself better than others. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Imagine you are joining a Ph D program in a relevant area at IIT Madras. Write an appropriate SOP”, in about 200 words. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iKR77w_881A&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a> Before you start, check your plan against the five rules above: stories; specific events, incidents and targets; no jargon, cliché, vagueness or ambiguity; simple words and sentences; brief and error-free.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"statement-of-purpose-simple-words-fewer-words\">Statement of purpose: simple words, fewer words</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Your life story would fill a book, but an application form gives you perhaps 200 words. “You always have to say a lot in a little.” Everyone works within limits of time and space: films, books, abstracts of research papers, classes. So we must build “an auto clock, a bio clock” that tells us: no more time, no more words. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a> The day you spilt milk on the bed, and what your mother taught you, could take 50 words alone. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The SOP is therefore <strong>also a test of language</strong>. The reader sees what you say and <strong>how</strong> you say it: “Are you careful with words? Can you distinguish between useful words and useless words? ... Do you use two words where others use three, or are you the one who uses four words at places where two words would suffice?” You pass this test only by practice in writing, not by listening to lectures. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"write-for-every-reader\">Write for every reader</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=339s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:39</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>An SOP, ideally, must be written in the simplest possible language.</li>\n<li>It is a story, not a research paper in a scientific journal.</li>\n<li>Avoid all cliché and jargon. Don’t say redundant things, ‘There was a monkey’ and ‘it had a tail’.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A research paper is read by your <strong>peer group</strong> (people trained in your subject). An SOP may also be read by admissions officers, consultants from other disciplines and managers who never studied your subject. Their jargon is different, and “the same words might have different meanings in their field”. So: simple language, stories, and no redundancy. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Redundant</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A word or a detail is <strong>redundant</strong> when it tells the reader nothing new. “There was a monkey; it had a tail”: every monkey has a tail, so the second part is redundant. “A monkey had a horn” would be news, so say it. Cows, bulls and rhinos have horns; lions, tigers and dogs do not: “if you had a dog with a horn, please say that.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"great-writers-use-simple-words\">Great writers use simple words</h3>\n<p>The opening of Jean-Paul Sartre’s autobiography, <em>The Words</em> (in French, <em>Les Mots</em>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=474s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:54</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Around 1850, in Alsace, a school teacher with more children than he could afford was willing to become a grocer. This unfrocked clerk wanted compensation. Since he was giving up the schooling of minds, one of his sons would school souls.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The next slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=555s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:15</a>: “This is the story of the family of Jean Paul Sartre (1905-1980), an eminent French writer. There is only one uncommon word here, unfrocked. All great stories, be it the <em>Holy Bible</em>, the <em>Ramayana</em> or any other are told in simple language.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alsace</strong> is a French province on the borders of Germany and Switzerland.</li>\n<li>In one sentence of everyday words we see a lower-middle-class family with “more people than food”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Unfrocked</strong>: (of a priest) removed from his post; a <em>frock</em> is a priest’s long robe. Here the teacher who gives up teaching is compared, as a joke, to a priest who leaves the church. “One of his sons would <strong>school souls</strong>”: a son would teach religion (become a priest).</li>\n<li>The lecturer’s list of texts in simple language: the Bible, Shakespeare, Milton, the Ramayana, the Mahabharata, the Panchatantra, the Upanishads, the Holy Quran. They are written “for the world”, not for one group. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-simple-language-is\">What simple language is</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=555s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:15</a>: “It has simple sentences and simple words. A sentence with only one verb is a simple sentence, a one or two-syllable word with well-known meaning is a simple word. These two make a simple language.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>syllable</strong> is one beat of a word: <em>rich</em> has one, <em>wealth-y</em> two, <em>af-flu-ent</em> three.</li>\n<li>The lecturer’s example: do not say <em>in the foreseeable future</em> or <em>in my perspective</em>; say <strong>as far as I can see</strong>: six words of one syllable each, all in everyday use. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></li>\n<li>“Writing simple language is not easy”: we are used to hearing and speaking in an “un-simple” manner, so learning it takes time, and the time is worth it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n<li>“One verb” means one clause: compare the simple sentences of <a href=\"#/english2/w03/independent-clauses-and-simple-sentences\">Week 3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which sentence is the simplest? (the lecture’s question at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=795s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>a. My parents were not rich.\nb. My parents were not wealthy.\nc. My parents were not affluent.\nd. I was born in a family which was not very affluent.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Count the verbs. (a), (b) and (c) have one verb, <em>were</em>. (d) has two, <em>was born</em> and <em>was</em>, so it is not a simple sentence.</li>\n<li>Compare the key words: <em>rich</em> (one syllable) is more common than <em>wealthy</em> (two), which is more common than <em>affluent</em> (three).</li>\n<li>Only (a) has one verb <strong>and</strong> only common words.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a). The slide: “The first is the simplest. It has only one verb, and only common words.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=807s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:27</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Listen for the language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s second clip is the first three minutes of Steve Jobs’ 2005 Stanford commencement address. Watch it more than once: for the story (people from humble families can “make it big through hard work, determination and discipline”) and for the language, because “all great stories are almost always told in simple language”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=732s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:12</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"uncommon-words-and-familiar-words\">Uncommon words and familiar words</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>My journey of self exploration began well before I can recall. I watched science fiction movies about robots and extraterrestrials and became enthralled by the idea of a machine with emotions. When I was 16 I discovered my inspiration for poetry. Poetry was to me a longing for what lies beyond pedestrian reality. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=876s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:36</a>: “There is hardly a simple sentence. All keywords, such as ‘self-exploration’, ‘watched science-fiction movies about robot and extra terrestrials and became enthralled...’ are uncommon words.” Different readers would take “my journey of self-exploration” in different ways, and the writer may mean something else again. (<strong>Extraterrestrials</strong>: beings from outside the Earth; <strong>enthralled</strong>: fascinated; <strong>pedestrian</strong> here means ordinary, dull.)</p>\n<p>Compare this paragraph from a successful SOP, “where every word and every sentence is simple” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=903s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:03</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In college, we learnt a range of topics from the basics of Economics like Micro Economics, Macro Economics to those like ‘History of Economic Thought’ and ‘Fiscal Economics’. However, the focus was more on gaining bookish knowledge, and, so, though I understand the basics of Economics, I do not understand clearly the many ways in which economics touches our life. I want answers for questions like why there is emphasis on economic parameters like GDP, when this growth is never inclusive, why the development of some economies is hugely kinked, why is it so important to have a stable political economy for the economy to develop and so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Apart from the names of subjects, the words are familiar: <em>growth, never, why, development, hugely, kinked</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How long may a word be?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Monosyllabic, bisyllabic at the most. If necessary, trisyllabic as well”, but never “deliberately difficult, deliberately infrequent words, that takes you nowhere”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"fewer-words-cut-prune-redraft\">Fewer words: cut, prune, redraft</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=927s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:27</a>: “Similarly, we should not use unnecessary words. They are redundant. Story-telling need not take more words than allowed, but many people do not see the value of words. They often write more words than necessary.” Your whole life in 200 words is possible, “but it is only that you may have to do more than one draft”: cut, prune, and also add, until both you and the word limit are satisfied. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a> (<a href=\"#/english2/w04/cutting-to-the-word-limit\">Week 4</a> cut an essay to its word limit in the same way.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Seventeen years of teaching (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=996s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:36</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The sentence (23 words): “My seventeen years of teaching experience and the ability to lead and control various departments can surely add value to your esteemed organization.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“Do we need ‘my’ before seventeen years?” No: say it with a verb, <em>I taught for seventeen years</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Lead and control</em>: one of the two would do.</li>\n<li><em>Surely</em> adds nothing.</li>\n<li><em>Add value to your esteemed organization</em> is a cliché: say what you can do.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1074s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:54</a>: “I taught for seventeen years. I can work with different kinds of people.” (13 words.) The lecturer’s spoken version: “I have taught for 17 years, I have worked in various departments, and I can be useful to you.” In the next sentence, <em>At present I am working at the Leo Academy</em> becomes <strong>Presently, I teach at Leo Academy</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s list of redundant, vague and contradictory phrases, with the slide’s comments <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1131s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:51</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>The problem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>a valuable asset</em></td>\n<td>“If it is an asset it is valuable.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>young and energetic</em></td>\n<td>adds little: are not all young people energetic?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>independent researcher</em> and <em>a team player</em></td>\n<td>“How can you have both?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>complete dedication</em></td>\n<td>“Dedication is enough.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>enjoy challenging assignments</em></td>\n<td>“What are they?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>prove myself</em></td>\n<td>“To whom and for what?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>English Post-Graduate</em></td>\n<td>ambiguous: nationality, language or literature?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The slide’s own sentence, “Many of these words add little to their sentence”, can be cut too: <strong>Many words add little to a sentence</strong> (9 words → 7). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a> On the team player, the lecturer adds that a good team member “speaks last, who collects all opinions and then who comes up with the best”, not one who starts first in an arrogant manner. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-activity-what-can-go\">The lecture’s activity: what can go?</h3>\n<p>“Look at the following examples and identify all those words which can be easily taken away without affecting the meaning.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1269s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:09</a> The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1293s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:33</a> has three paragraphs from real SOPs: why the writer wants a PhD, why IIT Gandhinagar (<em>IITGN</em>), and the <em>journey of self exploration</em> paragraph above, with a few more sentences. Pause the video there to practise on all of them. The lecturer works through four sentences:</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecturer’s own cuts (at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>“I wish to pursue Phd to get a decent job. With ever increasing unemployment in our country, a Phd from a reputed institute will allow me to survive without struggling economically.” (31 words.) It says: a PhD will help me get a better-paid job. The candidate is frank, and that is fine, “but must we take so many words?” A <em>reputed institute</em> only means a good one, and a good PhD has to be earned anyway. Rewrite: <strong>A PhD can get me a better job.</strong> (8 words.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></li>\n<li>“It will make me adept at finding details that have been missed.” (12 words.) Rewrite: <strong>A PhD can train me to find missing details.</strong> (9 words.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a></li>\n<li>“IITGN is a premier institute and it can provide me the resources to make an impactful study.” Read with the full name, Indian Institute of Technology Gandhinagar, it is about 20 words. “What is premier?” Premier compared with which places? Rewrite: <strong>IIT Gandhinagar can help me get facilities for research.</strong> (9 words.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> each sentence keeps its meaning in about half the words, or fewer. Try the rest of the slide yourself, and redraft “as many times as you can”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Short is not sloppy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Writing in fewer words does not mean writing sloppy or writing words that do not communicate.” Cut words “without losing on meaning, without losing clarity”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aUWOaB8M5sc&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"pre-cis-what-it-is-and-the-two-rules\">Précis: what it is and the two rules</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The SOP lectures asked you to say a lot in a few words. A <strong>précis</strong> turns this into an exact exercise: say <strong>everything</strong> a passage says, in <strong>one-third</strong> of its words. Many people call it “the ultimate skill” and “the ultimate test” in a language, because no other test looks at so many areas of a language in so little time. That is why many proficiency tests in English include it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=45s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:45</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Précis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>précis</strong> is a short, exact summary of a passage. The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=291s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:51</a>: “A good précis says all that has been said in the given passage; but the précis must say that in one-third of all the words in the original passage.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Say it right: PRAY-see</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <em>s</em> in <em>précis</em> is silent. The word has two syllables, with the stress on the first: <strong>PRAY-see</strong> /ˈpreɪsiː/. The slide: “It is not /precis/, it is /preci/ with stress on initial syllable.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why is it hard? Because it needs three skills at once <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reading</strong>: understand the passage well, “not just get the gist of it, even get the nuances of it”: what is said, how it is said, and what the author intends. (The <strong>gist</strong> is the main idea; <strong>nuances</strong> are small differences of meaning.) Speed in reading helps.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing</strong>: good grammar, so that you can put the same ideas into a new sentence pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>Words</strong>: knowing that a word “can be used also in another manner”, and that one word can do the work of many.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The two rules of précis writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Comprehend the passage well.</strong> Read it carefully and make a point-wise list of the important things it says: miss nothing important, take nothing unimportant. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Write it within one-third of the original words.</strong> “All else follows.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>To find the word limit, divide the number of words by 3. When the answer is not a whole number, the lecture rounds it up for the limit, and calls the whole number below it the ideal.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Words in the passage</th>\n<th>Divide by 3</th>\n<th>Limit in the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>99</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">99</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">33</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>34</td>\n<td><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">34</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11.3</span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>12 (ideal 11)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>49</td>\n<td><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">49</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16.3</span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>17 (ideal 16)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>153</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">153</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">51</span></span></span></span></span></td>\n<td>51</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"jefferson-in-12-words-the-first-example\">Jefferson in 12 words: the first example</h3>\n<p>The passage <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=375s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:15</a>, from an essay the lecture attaches:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thomas Jefferson is best known for the Declaration of Independence, in which he announced the separation of the 13 British colonies in North America — the right of Americans in these places to govern themselves. (34 words)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">From 34 words to 12 (the lecture’s drafts at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=624s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:24</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>List the points</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=417s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:57</a>): (a) Thomas Jefferson wrote the Declaration of Independence (DoI). (b) The DoI announced the separation of 13 British colonies in North America. (c) The DoI declared the right of the Americans to govern themselves.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the limit:</strong> <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">34</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11.3</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> so about 12 words, “without missing a point”.</li>\n<li><strong>First draft</strong> (19 words): <em>Thomas Jefferson announced the separation of the 13 British colonies of North America and their right to govern themselves.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Second draft</strong> (17 words): <em>Thomas Jefferson declared the 13 British colonies of North America were independent. They would henceforth govern themselves.</em> (<strong>Henceforth</strong> means “from now on”.)</li>\n<li><strong>Third draft</strong> (14 words on the slide; I count 13): <em>The 13 British American colonies were independent to govern themselves, declared Thomas Jefferson.</em> Now <em>British colonies of North America</em> (5 words) has become <em>British American colonies</em> (3).</li>\n<li><strong>Final draft</strong> (12 words): <em>The 13 British American colonies would henceforth govern themselves, declared Thomas Jefferson.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Check the points:</strong> (a) <em>declared Thomas Jefferson</em>; (b) colonies that “would henceforth govern themselves” are no longer ruled by Britain, so the separation is there; (c) <em>govern themselves</em>. All three are kept.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>The 13 British American colonies would henceforth govern themselves, declared Thomas Jefferson.</em> (12 words; the numeral <em>13</em> counts as one word.) “This 12 words sentence does all that we said in a long paragraph above.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not mindless cutting</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Should it be mindless cutting of words? No, the passage should be rewritten using words as they fit, in the new grammatical pattern.” Look at the final draft: the sentence is turned around, and <em>declared Thomas Jefferson</em> moves to the end. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-longer-passage-153-words-into-51\">A longer passage: 153 words into 51</h3>\n<p>The next part of the essay <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=717s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:57</a> (the lecturer calls it “about 156 or so” words; I count 153):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thomas Jefferson is best known for the Declaration of Independence, in which he announced the separation of the 13 British colonies in North America — the right of Americans in these places to govern themselves. He should also be known for declaring another kind of independence: the sovereignty of the Americans living at any one time over their own affairs. In an earlier lecture, we have already heard Jefferson urge us to ‘consider each generation as a distinct nation, with a right, by the will of its majority, to bind themselves, but none to bind the succeeding generation, more than the inhabitants of another country’. ‘The dead have no right,’ he added. ‘They are nothing; and nothing cannot own something … This corporeal globe, and everything upon it, belongs to its present corporeal inhabitants, during their generation.’ This way of thinking gave Jefferson’s generation the courage to do the unprecedented — to found a new nation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Words to know: <strong>sovereignty</strong>, the power to rule yourself; <strong>to bind</strong>, to tie someone by a rule or a promise; the <strong>succeeding</strong> generation, the one that comes next; <strong>corporeal</strong>, having a body, physical; <strong>unprecedented</strong>, never done before; <strong>to found</strong>, to start (a nation or an institution).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">153 words into 51 (the lecture’s drafts at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The points</strong> (the slide): (a) 13 British American colonies are independent. (b) They would govern themselves. (c) Each generation has its rights over its own affairs. (d) It cannot be bound by the preceding generation, nor can it bind the following generations. (e) The dead have no rights. (f) This philosophy helped the Americans found a new nation. (The slide writes “find”; the passage says “found”.)</li>\n<li><strong>The limit:</strong> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">153</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">51</span></span></span></span></span> words.</li>\n<li><strong>First draft</strong> (66 words) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=804s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:24</a>: <em>Thomas Jefferson wrote the declaration of independence of America. He said that 13 British American colonies are independent. They would henceforth govern themselves. Just as these colonies would not be bound by another country, they would not be bound by another generation; nor would they bind another generation. The declaration of independence announced the dead had no rights, and made the creation of new nation possible.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Too long by:</strong> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">66</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">51</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span></span></span></span></span> words.</li>\n<li><strong>Final draft</strong> (51 words) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=846s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:06</a>: <em>Declaring that the 13 British American colonies were independent, Thomas Jefferson said they would henceforth govern themselves. He had already said that no generation bound or was bound by another generation. Another country and another generation had no right on the present. The new American nation was created by this philosophy.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Where the 15 words went</strong> (my count): the first three sentences (23 words) became one sentence that begins <em>Declaring that …</em> (17 words), saving 6; the middle part was rewritten in the same 25 words, but now also covers point (e): another generation, the dead, “had no right on the present”; the last sentence went from 18 words to 9, saving 9. Total saved: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the final draft keeps all six points in exactly 51 words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-pre-cis-is-a-paragraph\">A précis is a paragraph</h3>\n<p>The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=936s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:36</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li>A good précis must not read like isolated sentences listed next to bullet points.</li>\n<li>It must be one or more paragraph with sentences following from and leading to one another.</li>\n<li>At the same time all this must be done within one-third of the words in the original passage.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A paragraph centres on one point; “if the point changes, the paragraph ought to change”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a> A précis grows in stages: read, understand, write a first draft. The first draft may be too long and the second a little shorter, but “your final draft must be within the stipulated limit”. You may read one day and write the next. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activities</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Activity 1: read the whole essay (the lecture gives a link). It “talks about the rights of the dead, just as we concede the rights of the unborn”. Activity 2: following the same steps, make a précis of the entire essay. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k7H0g4DdLrQ&amp;t=1002s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:42</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"pre-cis-the-techniques\">Précis: the techniques</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The rules say <em>what</em> a précis must do. This lecture shows <em>how</em> to handle the language so that everything fits into one-third of the words. The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=72s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:12</a>: “Précis-writing demands good proficiency in the language. On the whole, one must have a good sense of language.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"step-1-read-and-list-the-points\">Step 1: read and list the points</h3>\n<p>“There is no substitute for hard work”: read the passage very carefully. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a> The passage <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=96s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:36</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In our time, we have had revealed to us a new and wider meaning for Jefferson’s principle of the sovereignty of the present generation. The sovereign present has become the imperial present, dominating our knowledge and our concerns, reaching out with menacing power over vast areas of our consciousness. (49 words)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(<strong>Imperial</strong>: like an empire, ruling over others; <strong>menacing</strong>: threatening; <strong>consciousness</strong>: everything we are aware of.)</p>\n<p>The important points <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=129s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:09</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>New meaning of Jefferson’s principle: sovereignty of the present generation.</li>\n<li>Effect of the new meaning: solely concerned with the present.</li>\n<li>Its menacing power.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The limit <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=147s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:27</a>: “The given passage has 49 words, so its précis should have no more than 17 words.” <span class=\"nwp\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">49</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">16.3</span></span></span></span>.)</span></span></p>\n<h3 id=\"step-2-seven-techniques\">Step 2: seven techniques</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The principles of précis writing (a to g)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>From the slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=147s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:27</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a. Remove redundant words.</li>\n<li>b. Use one word for many words.</li>\n<li>c. Re-write sentences, if necessary, to save words.</li>\n<li>d. Re-write in indirect speech, changing all pronouns into third person but without changing their number and gender. So <em>we</em> may become <em>they</em>; <em>you</em> and <em>I</em> may become <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, <em>it</em> or <em>they</em>, depending upon number and gender.</li>\n<li>e. All illustrations and examples, or long lists of names and particulars, need not be there in the précis.</li>\n<li>f. But sentences must be connected, if necessary, with discourse markers like <em>also, besides, but, hence, henceforth, otherwise, if, so, therefore, thus</em>, keeping their numbers under control.</li>\n<li>g. Except a single-digit number, other numbers are given best in numerals rather than in words.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Notes on three of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(d) Indirect speech.</strong> <em>Direct speech</em> gives the speaker’s own words; <em>indirect</em> (reported) speech reports them from outside, in the third person and with the tenses in agreement. In the first draft below, <em>we have had revealed to us</em> becomes <em>they have found</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>(f) Discourse markers</strong> are the linking words of <a href=\"#/english2/w01/discourse-markers\">Week 1</a>. In a précis they “should be there only when they would make the passage easier to read”, not “for their own sake”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>(g) Numbers.</strong> For a single digit it does not matter: <em>six</em> and <em>6</em> are both one word. But <em>36</em> is one word, while the lecturer counts <em>thirty-six</em> as two; and <em>100,000</em> is one word, while <em>one hundred thousand</em> is three. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=348s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:48</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same tools as an essay</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Remove redundant words, use one word for many, rewrite the sentence: these are the cuts that <a href=\"#/english2/w04/cutting-to-the-word-limit\">Week 4</a> used on an essay. Together they remove “two-thirds of the original words and yet say the same thing in a very coherent manner”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"redundant-words-and-one-word-for-many\">Redundant words and one word for many</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s tables <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=393s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:33</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=507s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:27</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In the passage</th>\n<th>Shorter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>In our time</td>\n<td>Presently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>have had revealed to us</td>\n<td>(have) seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>new and wider meaning</td>\n<td>new meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dominating our knowledge and concern</td>\n<td>dominating us</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reaching out with menacing power over vast areas of</td>\n<td>threatening</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Dominating us</em> covers “everything within us”: our knowledge, our concerns, our whole body and mind. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li><em>Reaching out with menacing power over vast areas of</em> is “all right for rhetoric” (language meant to impress), but in a précis one word does: <em>threatening</em> (or <em>intimidating</em>, <em>frightening</em>). That is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span></span> words replaced by one. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-drafts-49-words-into-17\">The drafts: 49 words into 17</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three drafts (the lecture’s drafts at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=522s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:42</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>First draft</strong> (principles c and d; 32 words): <em>In the present times, they have found a new meaning for Jefferson’s sovereignty principle of the present-generation. The sovereign present has become the imperial present, dominating and threatening our areas of consciousness.</em> The pronoun <em>we</em> has become <em>they</em>, and nine words have become <em>threatening</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Second draft</strong> (25 words) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=588s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:48</a>: <em>Jefferson’s sovereignty principle for the present generation has seen a new meaning. This has become the imperial present, dominating and threatening our areas of consciousness.</em> The principle is now the subject of the first sentence, so <em>In the present times, they have found</em> goes, and <em>The sovereign present</em> becomes <em>This</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Still too long:</strong> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">17</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span></span> words must go “without losing meaning”.</li>\n<li><strong>Third draft</strong> (17 words) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=645s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:45</a>: <em>Sovereignty of the present has become the imperial present, pervading and menacing entire areas of our consciousness.</em> (<strong>Pervading</strong>: spreading through every part.)</li>\n<li><strong>Check:</strong> 17 words is within the limit, and the meaning stays: the present generation is so taken up with the present that it lets the present rule its whole mind; nothing past and nothing to come matters to it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 49 words → 32 → 25 → 17, “and we have not lost anything of the meaning”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"long-lists-of-illustrations\">Long lists of illustrations</h3>\n<p>The slide “Illustrations and discourse markers” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=693s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:33</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We have seen how aeronautics and electronics gave to spatial dimensions a disorienting new irrelevance. Americans, unsure whether what they viewed on television was actually happening in Chicago, in Los Angeles, in New York, or in a nearby studio, were newly uncertain about the where of events. They would be no less confused about the when. Candid photography, the hand-held motion picture camera, the novel powers of kinescoping and videotaping — all these would affect the viewer’s sense of time with a new ambiguity. Was what you watched actually happening while you watched? (92 words)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(<strong>Aeronautics</strong>: the science of flight; <strong>spatial dimensions</strong>: distances in space; <strong>kinescoping</strong>: an old way of recording a television picture by filming the screen.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">92 words into one short paragraph (the lecture’s final draft at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=735s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Drop the list (e):</strong> <em>in Chicago, in Los Angeles, in New York, or in a nearby studio</em> (13 words). For a television audience it does not matter where, so the précis says <strong>here or there</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>One word for many (b):</strong> <em>aeronautics and electronics</em> (3 words) becomes <strong>science</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Final draft</strong> (the slide says 32 words): <em>We have seen how science has rendered distances meaningless. For television audience, it does not matter whether an event happened here or there. The new technology gave them the illusion of ever present.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Check the limit:</strong> <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">92</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.7</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> so the target is about 31 words. By my count the draft has 33 words, or 32 if <em>ever-present</em> is written as one word. It is close; as practice, try to cut one or two more words.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the draft keeps both ideas: science made distance meaningless (the <em>where</em>), and new recording technology made viewers unsure of the <em>when</em> (“the illusion of ever present”).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Thus through two or three drafts and following some principles of language, that same thing can be said in fewer words.” Perfection does not come “by only listening to your teacher”; it comes “only through our own hard work”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a> The activity: “Make an analysis of the rest of the essay in the same manner.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BT8OixxCEU4&amp;t=804s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:24</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-a-lab-report\">Writing a lab report</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <a href=\"#/english2/w04/writing-a-lab-manual\">Week 4</a> a <strong>lab manual</strong> told a worker, step by step, how to make tea, “leaving nothing to guesswork, causing no confusion”. But when the work is done, “actually the work does not end there. All work in laboratory is followed by a report.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A laboratory may be rich or poor, big or small: a school science lab, an engineering workshop, or a huge research centre like CERN or NASA, where very many people work on projects that last for decades. All of them follow “a very clearly precisely stated procedure” (the manual), and all of them expect certain results. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a> The slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=414s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:54</a>, with a photo of a school lab and one of a research lab: “All experiments in all laboratories are followed by reports.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One purpose of a school lab is to <strong>demonstrate scientific truths</strong>. Does water boil at 100 degrees centigrade? Does water evaporate in sunlight? Soak a piece of paper, leave it in the sun, and after a while it is dry. How long that takes depends on the intensity of the sunlight, the amount of water, and where the paper is kept. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></li>\n<li>Other experiments <strong>try things out</strong>: “if we put this way, how is it going to go? if we put that way, how is it going to go?” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></li>\n<li>Fieldwork by a social scientist or a manager, and a process simulated in a computer lab, are experiments too. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"manual-before-report-after\">Manual before, report after</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Lab manual and lab report</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every experiment has two kinds of writing <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>manual</strong>, written <strong>before</strong> the work: what is to be done and how it can be done;</li>\n<li>the <strong>report</strong>, written <strong>after</strong> the work: what was done, how it was done, whether it succeeded, whether it gave the expected result or a different one, or whether it failed. “It must be recorded so that other researchers, other workers in the laboratory learn from these experiments.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“If it precedes work, then it is a manual; if it follows work, then it is a report.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Lab manual</th>\n<th>Lab report</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>When</td>\n<td>before the work</td>\n<td>after the work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What it gives</td>\n<td>tools, time, duration and sequence, “in a general manner”</td>\n<td>a record of what was actually tried and done</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nature</td>\n<td>instructions</td>\n<td>“concrete, it is specific; it describes, it does not discuss”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentences</td>\n<td>commands: <em>Pour a cup of water into it.</em></td>\n<td>passive: <em>A cup and a quarter of water was poured into the percolator.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A report “can discuss results, but usually it describes what has happened”, in <strong>impersonal</strong> language: language “that is not as specific to a person or a place”. (<strong>Impersonal</strong>: not about the writer as a person.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember the order</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>M</strong>anual = <strong>m</strong>ap, drawn before the journey. <strong>R</strong>eport = <strong>r</strong>ecord, written after it. (My memory trick.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-report-answers\">What a report answers</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The questions a lab report answers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=459s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:39</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a. What was done</li>\n<li>b. Why it was done</li>\n<li>c. How it was done: (i) tools, (ii) methods, (iii) materials, (iv) process, (v) any other relevant information</li>\n<li>d. Time and cost</li>\n<li>e. Results</li>\n<li>f. Previous work, differences and similarities</li>\n<li>g. Applications, extensions, future work</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Previous work</strong> means experiments others have done before; <strong>extensions</strong> are ways to take the work further.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-report-making-tea\">The lecture’s report: making tea</h3>\n<p>Tea is made differently across Asia: in China, Southeast Asia, Korea and Japan there are “little local differences”. In India many people add milk and sugar, some boil the leaves, and some make spice tea with ginger or tulsi leaves. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a> The lecture shows a short video, “How to make green tea the right way” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>, and then the slide <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=678s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:18</a>: “You may have noticed that even an everyday thing like ‘making a cup of tea’ is also an experimental project in a lab. All that we have said about a report could as well be said about making tea.” “To keep matters simple”, the report describes not the video but the tea from Week 4’s manual. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<p>The report, as on the slides <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=738s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:18</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=768s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:48</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=873s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:33</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1059s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:39</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>A report on making tea</strong></p>\n<p>a. <strong>What was done:</strong> Green Tea was made for one.</p>\n<p>b. <strong>Why it was done:</strong> To welcome a visitor, as a mark of hospitality.</p>\n<p>c. <strong>How it was done</strong></p>\n<p>(i) <strong>Tools:</strong> Percolator, Cup and Saucer, Spoon, Unbroken Tea Leaves (Half a teaspoon), Honey, Strainer</p>\n<p>(ii) <strong>Methods:</strong> A cup and a quarter of water was poured into the percolator. Percolator was switched on and time noted. 40 seconds later water started boiling. Percolator, on thermostat, switched itself off after 40 seconds.</p>\n<p>(iii) <strong>Materials:</strong> Tea leaves (Half a teaspoon); Honey, one teaspoon; Water, 77.93 ml</p>\n<p>(iv) <strong>Process:</strong> The process involved the following: warming of solid objects; boiling of liquids; soaking of one substance by another; stirring.</p>\n<p>(v) <strong>Any other relevant information:</strong> This is not a new experiment, but a demonstration will prove that best tea can be got even today only with the traditional material in a traditional way. (A link is given for more.)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What the lecturer says about it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tools.</strong> A percolator (here, a pot that boils water; traditionally a kettle; many people now use a pan, which is easier to clean, but a kettle boils water better), a cup and saucer, a small teaspoon and a strainer. Unbroken tea leaves are “very important” for very good tea. Milk and sugar, lime or lemon, ginger and other spices can be added to the list, to suit your taste. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a> A <strong>thermostat</strong> switches a heater off at a set temperature.</li>\n<li><strong>Materials before or after the process?</strong> Either: “it can also come afterwards because it is a report and the report follows work, does not precede work.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a> (The slide’s list of tools also names the tea leaves and honey. Strictly, those are materials: tools are kept, materials are used up. That is why they appear again under (iii). My note.)</li>\n<li><strong>Water.</strong> “You do not need more than 75 milliliters of water”, but 2 or 3 ml more is left in the percolator, so that its hot plate does not burn under its own heat: hence 77.93 ml. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Process.</strong> <em>Warming of solid objects</em>: the cup and saucer are warmed (hot, but not too hot to touch), so that the tea does not pass “suddenly into a very cold medium” and lose its flavour. <em>Boiling of liquids</em>: the water. <em>Soaking</em>: the tea leaves soak up the water. <em>Stirring</em>: the honey is added and stirred in. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Checking the tea report against the seven questions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>a, b</strong> (what, why): answered: green tea for one, to welcome a visitor.</li>\n<li><strong>c</strong> (how): all five parts are there: tools, methods, materials, process, other information.</li>\n<li><strong>d</strong> (time and cost): time is there (the water boiled 40 seconds after switching on); cost is not given.</li>\n<li><strong>e</strong> (results): not on the slides. The lecturer adds it aloud: the tea is ready to drink “when it changes color. From plain watery, it becomes greenish-yellow or greenish-red”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>f</strong> (previous work): the lecturer mentions books, reports and manuals on tea making, and says this tea “is not entirely traditional either” because honey is added. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>g</strong> (applications, future work): not given.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the tea report answers a, b and c fully, and d, e and f in part. When you write your own report, answer all seven questions.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-language-of-a-report\">The language of a report</h3>\n<p>“Nearly all sentences are simple sentences, but they are all in passive voice and in the past tense. You can also write in the present tense, that doesn’t matter; but it should be in the passive voice.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why the passive voice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An experiment can be “replicated regardless by anyone, regardless of who does it”. The report’s claim is: if you follow this procedure, with these materials and these tools, you will get these results. Who did the work does not matter, so the doer is left out: the <strong>passive voice</strong> puts the thing acted on first (<em>Green tea was made</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a> (How passives are built: <a href=\"#/english2/w02/passives-how-they-are-built\">Week 2</a>.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Personal, active (my versions)</th>\n<th>Report style (the slides)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I poured a cup and a quarter of water into the percolator.</td>\n<td>A cup and a quarter of water was poured into the percolator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We switched on the percolator and noted the time.</td>\n<td>Percolator was switched on and time noted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We made green tea for one.</td>\n<td>Green Tea was made for one.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learning is an attitude</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“I have not joined this course to learn how to make tea”? The lesson is wider: “we are also learning how to write about things we do every day and we take them for granted ... the opportunities are all around you, if you take care and if you look around.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The activity <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1131s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:51</a>: “Write a similar report on making coffee / making <em>rasam</em> / some ethnic dish.” A dish from any culture will do (from Vietnam, Nigeria, Kenya, Gabon, Senegal or the Andamans), because “a report can still follow the same structure”: what was done, how, the tools, methods and materials, what was expected and what happened. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXaf1DGYAus&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SOP</strong> (8.1): marks and degrees tell what you passed; testimonials tell how others see you; the SOP tells your story, the reasons behind your successes and failures. Grades get you shortlisted; the SOP decides among A graders.</li>\n<li><strong>A good SOP</strong> tells stories; describes specific events, incidents and targets; avoids jargon, cliché, vagueness and ambiguity; uses simple words and simple sentences; is brief and error-free. Show, do not label. Include what you learnt from family, sports, friends and teachers. Be honest, frank and humble: neither boast nor hide.</li>\n<li><strong>A or an before an abbreviation</strong>: by the first sound: <em>an SOP, an NGO, a PhD</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple language</strong> (8.2) = simple sentences (one verb) + simple words (one or two syllables, well-known meaning). Readers are not only your peers. Remove <strong>redundant</strong> words (<em>a valuable asset</em>, <em>complete dedication</em>), contradictions (<em>independent researcher</em> and <em>team player</em>) and ambiguities (<em>English postgraduate</em>). Write two or three drafts: fewer words, with no loss of meaning or clarity.</li>\n<li><strong>Précis</strong> (8.3), said PRAY-see: says all that the passage says, in one-third of its words (limit = words ÷ 3, rounded up). Rule 1: comprehend and list the points. Rule 2: write within one-third. Rewrite, do not just cut; the result is a connected paragraph, not bullet points.</li>\n<li><strong>Précis techniques</strong> (8.4): (a) remove redundant words; (b) one word for many; (c) rewrite sentences; (d) indirect speech, third person, same number and gender; (e) drop illustrations and long lists; (f) connect with a few discourse markers; (g) numerals for numbers above 9. Draft until within the limit (49 → 32 → 25 → 17).</li>\n<li><strong>Lab report</strong> (8.5): the manual comes before the work, the report after it. A report answers: what was done, why, how (tools, methods, materials, process, other information), time and cost, results, previous work, applications and future work. It is concrete, specific and impersonal; it describes rather than discusses; it uses simple sentences in the passive voice, in the past (or present) tense, so that anyone can repeat the experiment.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is it “a SOP” or “an SOP”? And “a NGO” or “an NGO”? Give the reason.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>an SOP</strong> and <strong>an NGO</strong>. Choose <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> by the first <strong>sound</strong>. We read these abbreviations letter by letter: S is “ess” and N is “en”, and both begin with a vowel sound. (If SOP were read as one word, like <em>soap</em>, it would be <em>a SOP</em>.) By the same rule it is <strong>a PhD</strong>: P is “pee”, which begins with a consonant sound.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">An SOP says: “I am a hard-working person and I have always been curious.” What is wrong, and how does the lecture fix it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Both are <strong>labels</strong> without evidence. “Hard work” is subjective (three hours for one person, thirteen for another), and anyone could say “always curious”. The lecture’s fix is a specific number or a story:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“If I am awake for 18 hours, I like to work for 14.”</li>\n<li>“While I was still in the primary school, I went and asked my teacher why the rainbow would not appear every day.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which words add nothing in “a valuable asset”, “complete dedication” and “the ability to lead and control various departments”, and why?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>valuable</em>: an asset is valuable by definition.</li>\n<li><em>complete</em>: “dedication is enough”.</li>\n<li><em>lead and control</em>: one of the two would do.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All three are <strong>redundant</strong>: they tell the reader nothing new, so cutting them loses no meaning.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A passage has 87 words. What is the longest précis allowed, and what must it still contain? (My example.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Divide by 3: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">87</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">29</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>So the précis may have at most 29 words.</li>\n<li>It must still say <strong>all</strong> that the passage says: every important point on your list, written as one connected paragraph. A short text that drops a point is not a précis.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the technique in each change: (i) <em>In our time</em> → <em>Presently</em>; (ii) <em>in Chicago, in Los Angeles, in New York</em> → <em>here or there</em>; (iii) <em>we have had revealed to us</em> → <em>they have found</em>; (iv) <em>thirty-six</em> → <em>36</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(i) <strong>One word for many</strong> (b); it also removes redundant words (a).</li>\n<li>(ii) <strong>Dropping long lists of names and particulars</strong> (e).</li>\n<li>(iii) <strong>Rewriting in indirect speech</strong>, with the pronoun in the third person (d).</li>\n<li>(iv) <strong>Numerals for numbers above 9</strong> (g): <em>36</em> counts as one word.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Manual or report: (1) “Pour the hot water gently over the leaves.” (2) “The hot water was poured gently over the leaves.” Why is (2) in the passive?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(1) is from a <strong>manual</strong>: a command, written before the work, telling someone what to do. (2) is from a <strong>report</strong>: written after the work, in the past tense and the passive voice. The passive leaves out the doer because the doer does not matter: the claim is that anyone who follows the same procedure, with the same tools and materials, will get the same result.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
